{"book":{"id":"book-the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson","slug":"the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson","title":"The Lost Cities of Africa","authors":[{"id":"person-basil-davidson","name":"Basil Davidson"}],"isbn13":["9780316174312"],"subjects":["World History"],"topics":["Business, Capitalism & Corporations","Food, Agriculture & Land","Housing, Cities & Urban Life","Religion & Religious Movements"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=The%20Lost%20Cities%20of%20Africa%20Basil%20Davidson","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=The%20Lost%20Cities%20of%20Africa%20Basil%20Davidson","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=The%20Lost%20Cities%20of%20Africa%20Basil%20Davidson","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=The%20Lost%20Cities%20of%20Africa%20Basil%20Davidson","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2747605W"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-anisfield-wolf-nonfiction-anisfield-wolf-official-winners-archive","source-open-library-summary-the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson"],"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"synthesis","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"survey\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Africa","confidence":0.99},{"name":"Meroë","confidence":0.83},{"name":"Napata","confidence":0.78}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Archaeological evidence shows that Africa had highly developed, distinctly African civilizations, challenging the belief that the continent had no meaningful history.","confidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":43,"confidence":0.82},"profileConfidence":0.94,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"4e75e221f8654b101339a87a63d11953e480de7a275f032581d1dbebf505be7f","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"summary":"Sheba and Ophir, King Solomon’s mines, Timbuktu - for centuries the “Dark Continent” of Africa was a land of fabulous, golden legend. The European imagination invested it with great kingdoms and great wealth - a land ruled by a mysterious Christian king, Prester John. In the past two hundred years, however, these glittering legends have been replaced by a far different belief - that Africa is a land without a past, without history; that its peoples have always lived in savagery, in what has been described as “centuries-long stagnation.” The numerous and impressive archeological traces of earlier African civilizations have been ignored or attributed to a lost people. However, the truth is being found in the archeological record. There were civilizations, both highly developed and of purely African origin and character. In reality the great kingdom of Kush, with its splendid cities of Meroë and Napata, was an advanced African culture of the upper Nile several centuries before Christ. But the great flowering of African civilization south of the Sahara was medieval: the great kingdoms of Ghana, Mali, and Songhay; the merchant cities of the East African coast with a thriving Africa-India trade; and the mysterious states of the interior, like Zimbabwe and Mapungubwe. THE LOST CITIES OF AFRICA, by Basil Davidson, is a much-needed survey of what is presently known of the African past.”","publicationYear":1959,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Civilization","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Historia Da Africa","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-lost-cities-of-africa-basil-davidson"},{"source":"open_library","schem